A complete end-to-end diagnostic was performed across all four phases. Phase 1 (ICMP reachability sweep) showed zero packet loss on all sampled paths, including paths from both user hosts to the server subnet. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) found no ACL or firewall drop rules, no IP addressing errors, no static ARP poisoning, no duplicate MAC addresses, and all interfaces up on all 15 devices. Phase B (ospf_snapshot + tc_snapshot) confirmed the OSPF routing fabric is fully healthy — all six FRR routers have Full adjacencies, each carries 10 OSPF routes, and no traffic-control shaping or qdisc anomaly was detected on any interface. Phase D (service_snapshot + pressure_sweep) directly tested the reported symptom: `web1.local` resolved correctly to `10.200.0.4` (web_server_1's IP) on both `host_1_1_1_1` and `host_2_1_1_1`, HTTP requests to `http://web1.local/` returned HTTP 200 in ~4 ms from `host_1_1_1_1`, `web_server_1` answered its own localhost HTTP probe with 200 in under 1 ms, and no CPU pressure or stress tools were found on any device. There is no current fault, no historical trace (no flap scripts, no OSPF instability, no service failure logs), and no mechanism that could explain the reported outage. The portal "web1.local" is fully operational and `web_server_1` does not need a reboot. Root cause: the reported outage is unconfirmed — no fault exists in the live topology at time of investigation. No corrective action is warranted; users should retry and escalate only if a specific reproducible failure reappears.
